ting to work with  additional devices and designs and  cabinets you may be aware of larger  instruments that need to go into bscs  that are doing critical steps and  functions in the biosafety cabinet for  instance here is an automated bioreactor  and it is connected through lots of  different  passers and cords to support all the  complexities of the cabinet  and the uh and the bioreactor itself  while still allowing for good airflow  and  the ergonomics are still maintained for  a user  some of you may also be working with  samples that are for human health  and are going to be injected so cell  therapies or gene therapies or other  sterile products that are processed  in accordance with the gmps  in doing so you may have additional  requirements for monitoring  in this case there is an isokinetic  probe for air sampling that collects air  from the work area and helps validate  the sterility of your product  in this design  a funnel can be added and integrated  into the biosafety cabinet rather than  running extra lines through the whole  work area which can be difficult to  clean  many of your processes may also include  digital laboratory notebooks or other  processes that are best displayed  on screens associated with the biosafety  cabinet either integrated into the back  wall or connected through a laptop arm  or a monitor arm and these are things  that you should work with closely with  your manufacturer because they really  influence  how little space you have in your  laboratory and how best you can maximize  the area that the bsc is taking up by  integrating  monitors and getting rid of things like  computer cards  those of you who are working with  instruments that are also quite a bit  larger  may also find that your bsc  is not big enough even if you do some  basic modifications to it and so on the  left we have  one such liquid handler that fits well  into a biosafety cabinet  but a liquid handler such as the  hamilton star may be too large for a  standard bsc and in fact this model  particularly the hamilton star is too  large for a regular bfc  so be aware of other conform  conforming products that help maintain  class 2 airflows and support automation  as well and so some of the formats that  are out there are are wide and diverse  and highly customized and expensive but  there are options that are very very  compatible with many different types of  liquid handlers cell sorters and other  instruments that are too large for  regular bsc  so these large enclosures are available  in  different formats but what you really  want to focus in on is understanding the  space requirements for instrument and  then working with a manufacturer who's  got a lot of experience closely  designing these products  testing them with instruments inside of  them and making sure that all of that is  maintaining class 2 protection for  personnel that's you the user for the  product that's hand being handled inside  the enclosure and at the same time  maintaining ergonomics safety and  comfort  so these are just a few of the selection  options that you have to consider both  with the type of the bsc  the the sustainability considerations  and cost savings for your facility  as they pertain to both uh operational  costs and safety costs and then also  being mindful of other ways to select  bscs that can be adapted to improve your  workflows and make your day easier in  the laboratory or wherever you might be  using a class 2 bsc and so with that i'm  going to turn it over to  questions that have come in during the  presentation and thanks again for  joining  thank you david for that informative  presentation it is now time to start our  live q a portion of the webinar down to  our audience if you have a question you  would like to ask please do so now just  use that ask a question box type it in  there click submit and we will answer as  many of your questions as we have time  for  okay david let's get started we have  lots of questions that have come in here  uh let's start with this one  why would i want to use a type c in  center type d  good question um so a type c cabinet is  going to be that cabinet that can work  for any type of application whether it's  covet samples or  it's ebola samples  all are suitable on the biological side  the difference really with the c is  is on the chemistry and chemical vapor  handling side  a c is going to be a lot easier to  integrate into a building it's got  flexibility in terms of  how much air it exhausts  how safe it is that are leaps and bounds  ahead of a type b cabinet so it's  providing the same level of protection  by having 100 exhausted air without a  lot of the hang-ups and hiccups that  type b2 cabinets can sometimes bring to  the party  thanks david  next question we work with tuberculosis  samples is an a2 okay for this type of  work  so  uh as a biosafety cabinet manufacturer  our our recommendation is always going  to be for you to consult most closely  with your biosafety officer or your ehs  office and understand the the facility  requirements and the local requirements  for handling any biohazard  now for something like tuberculosis this  is obviously a highly infectious  pathogenic  organism that we really don't want to  have anything to do with and we want to  make sure we handle it safely so  a biohazard  such as this  is not a chemical risk by itself and so  we want to make sure that we look at  does a cabinet have the ability to  contain and all class ubscs are going to  provide that same level of  biocontainment so an a2 is acceptable  you may just be aware of in especially  because we have lots of international  guests  your country may have specific  requirements for handling tuberculosis  in a vented  enclosure but there is no known benefits  to exhausting a bsc  for handling tuberculosis so  samples the common misconception  thanks for that answer david next  question we just bought a new liquid  handler can we still get a bio safety  cabinet for it  this is also a great question um  so thank you for asking  when you're  buying a liquid handler i have been in  this case before where you're very  excited about how fast it's going to  make your work and it's going to  automate your process so you can you  know go work on other more important  things than transferring liquids from  from a sample  through the different phases of a  process  it also means that you may have  forgotten to look at the bsc  and sounds like in this case this user  has done that  the answer is is really you want to look  closely at the liquid handler you have  collect all of the information about the  model and then go to a manufacturer of a  larger enclosure and understand  what types of options they have for for  containing that liquid handler so in the  case of um large enclosures such as the  ones that lab conco makes there are lots  of different options uh for for  different sizes and connections and  customizations that can support adapting  an enclosure to the liquid handler you  may have already bought  having said that do be aware that some  instruments do require very large or  sometimes even completely customized in  containment so most liquid handlers  that's not the case but if you have a  large robotics process with multiple  steps  may require additional conversation  right david we're going to try squeezing  in a few more questions we have quite a  few coming in  i do want to let our audience know that  any of the questions we are unable to  get to we will be answering them via  email to the  email address that you provided at the  time of registration all right david  what is the difference between a vented  and non-vented filter bsc for coded  testing  that's a good question um yeah so  so coveted testing by itself is  uh for testing of a pathogenic human  sample a clinical sample requires in the  us just a bfl2 containment and that  means to traditionally you're working  with that sample if it's still  pathogenic in a class 2 bse it could be  any one of the the types of class 2bses  that are out there so it could be  recirculating or vented to the outside  phobid is is a viral particulate right  so  it's a virus in nature so  as you're handling it the hepa filters  with the bsc are going to be entirely  responsible for capturing that that  zykovic sample the virus  and so there's really not a benefit at  all or even a requirement to venting a  class ubsc for working with covet  testing  the only situation where you may choose  to vent a copic processing bsc is if for  some reason you were also working with  additional chemicals or  or something smelly like a different  sample besides the covid sample maybe  maybe a fecal sample or you want to vent  the other route but inherently there is  no real requirement  with a bsc to vent it out for covid  david what cabinet do you recommend for  animal work  good question um so there are lots of  different types of enclosures for uh  safe bio hazardous  animal handling um many times if you  look at the offerings um that are out  there there are it depends on the  process that is occurring so it may be  that you are exchanging an animal from  cage to cage such as a mouse and there  are great transfer stations that are  designed for that type of work that  provides both user and sample protection  or animal protection  if you're working with surgeries or  doing a thing a little bit more critical  with that animal  then typically there are class two bio  safety cabinets that are specialty  designs for working with animals  the key design concept there is is  usually a larger sash opening to allow  for passing of smaller cages in and out  of the work area such as a 12-inch sash  opening  all right and it looks like we have time  for one more question  are class 2 b2 cabinets louder than  others i'm looking for a quiet cabinet  does that exist  that's like that's a great question um  so  i mentioned earlier that class two bscs  are driven by  uh a building exhaust blower so  internally in a b2 cabinet there is just  a smaller blower typically uh for  supplying air down over the work area  the remaining air is sucked out from the  building itself  and as that happens there is a  substantial volume  of air that is pulled through a b2  cabinet it's quite quite loud because  it's double the air of a type a2 or a  type c1 cabinet so  if you look at the manufacturer's specs  for all b2 cabinets they typically say  they're pretty quiet but that is because  they're measuring the volume the noise  intensity for the cabinet and rarely the  whole picture where they're looking at  the total exhaust volume so  really b2 cabins should be avoided for  many reasons  and  one of those extra extra reasons down  the road is that volume  um and you know temperamentalness that  they also bring with them  david thank you so much for all of this  information do you have any final  comments for our audience  i wanted to just again thank you all for  your time and your fantastic questions i  know there are lots of other questions  that did not get answered just during  our short time today and we will address  those directly afterwards for those of  you who have questions  if you have more information on bscs  that you're requesting or want  additional help  feel free to visit labconco.com and get  in touch with one of our several  specialists for biosafety cabinets  otherwise thanks so much again for  attending and have a great rest of your  day  thank you again david and just a  reminder to our audience today's webcast  can be viewed on demand and lab roots  will alert you via email when it is  available to replay we encourage you to  share that email with your colleagues  who may have missed today's live event a  big thank you again to david lazecha and  labconco and we hope you all have a  great day  you

